  The fork is the module system. Right now the browser side ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m) and the Node side ([38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m, CommonJS) are two
  separate worlds with no bridge, and picking one determines how every future form imports the ID — a decision that gets
  expensive to reverse once several files depend on it.

  - [1mES modules[0m — new [38;5;153msrc/visitor-id.js[39m with [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, forms
    [38;5;153mimport[39m it. Standard, no bundler, scales cleanly to many forms. Costs: module scripts won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so
    you'd need to serve the page (an [38;5;153mnpm start[39m with a static server); the existing CommonJS files in [38;5;153msrc/[39m stay separate
    or get converted.
  - [1mPlain global script[0m — new script tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m exposing [38;5;153mwindow.VisitorId[39m. Zero tooling, matches the current
    style exactly, still works by double-clicking the HTML. Costs: a global, load-order coupling, and no reuse from the
    Node/CommonJS side.
  - [1mDual CommonJS + global shim[0m — one file usable from both [38;5;153mrequire()[39m and the browser. Reusable everywhere with no build
    step. Costs: wrapper boilerplate that's disproportionate for one function.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — esbuild or Vite. Most headroom, but it's real tooling for a 64-line project.

  Expensive to reverse: this one, once several forms import it. Cheap: the storage key and the ID format.

  I'd recommend [1mES modules[0m — "other forms will need it later" is exactly the case plain globals degrade in, and the only
  real cost is needing to serve the page instead of opening the file.
